  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "ref",
      "title": "Authored visual-contract reference",
      "role": "other",
      "access": "read",
      "inspected": "Complete local authored fixture reference, all named sections. Explicitly fictional/non-empirical UI material, not a course or provider trial.",
      "limitations": [
        "Authored UI fixture facts establish only the intended test semantics."
      ],
      "used": true,
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "id": "ref-1",
          "location": "Section: Comparison",
          "text": "In this fictional archive exercise, a Text card has a written label and a written description; it has no source picture. An Image card has a written label and a supplied source picture; its description names what is visible. Both card types keep the reference ID. Compare the same dimensions: label, representation and reference identity."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-2",
          "location": "Section: Decisions",
          "text": "To route a card, first ask whether its label is readable. A readable label goes to the labeled collection. An unreadable label goes to manual review. Manual review is a terminal state for this diagram; do not invent an automatic successful outcome."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-3",
          "location": "Section: Cause and effect",
          "text": "The fictional archive's access switch controls whether a card can be opened. When the switch is disabled, opening is blocked. When it is enabled, the open action can display the card. This is an explicitly stipulated software rule, not a measured effect. Being labeled happens earlier in the example sequence but does not by itself cause access permission."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-4",
          "location": "Section: Hierarchy",
          "text": "The fictional collection contains Text cards and Image cards. A Text card contains its label and written description. An Image card contains its label and supplied picture. These are containment relationships, not a ranking of importance or evidence quality."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-5",
          "location": "Section: Image annotation reference",
          "text": "The separate authored-classroom-task.png is an unchanged existing UI fixture. It displays a written symbol-to-response rule and an outlined shape beside four unmarked answer choices. Its printed PSYC 101 course label is fictional fixture context. A neutral label may identify the top corner of the displayed shape. Use the actual inspected point from IMAGE-INSPECTION.json, not guessed image coordinates."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-6",
          "location": "Section: Timeline",
          "text": "The fictional scheduled session starts at 09:00 with Collect. At 09:10 it reaches Label. At 09:25 it reaches Check. On a numeric timeline measured in minutes after 09:00, these scheduled positions are 0, 10 and 25. An ordinal timeline may show Collect before Label before Check, but its spacing must not claim equal elapsed intervals. These are illustrative planned times, not empirical observations or proof that one event causes the next."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-7",
          "location": "Section: Set overlap",
          "text": "The fictional set Text-described contains Card A and Card C. The set Image-supported contains Card B and Card C. Therefore Card A belongs only to Text-described, Card B only to Image-supported, and Card C to their overlap. No relative population size, area, probability or prevalence is specified. Use a two-set membership diagram with no quantitative area claim."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-8",
          "location": "Section: Sequence strip",
          "text": "For the authored classroom task, the ordered instructions are: Read the written rule; Inspect the displayed shape; Apply the rule to choose a response. The second step may display the actual unchanged source image. The initial sequence must not disclose the chosen response. These are ordered task actions, not invented intermediate scientific pictures or measured durations."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-9",
          "location": "Section: Worked example",
          "text": "Problem: The fictional schedule reaches Label at 09:10 and Check at 09:25. How many minutes are scheduled between them? The solution identifies the same-hour minute values 10 and 25, subtracts 25 minus 10, and concludes 15 minutes. Reveal all solution steps and the final answer together after the problem. No answer belongs in the collapsed panel title, initial alt text or neutral setup."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-10",
          "location": "Section: Ordinal continuum",
          "text": "The fictional archive's readiness stages are Unsorted, Labeled and Checked, in that order. A folder can be at the Labeled stage. No equal interval, numeric score, scientific threshold or time-to-completion is established by these categories. Labeling a point on this ordinal continuum must not invent one."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-11",
          "location": "Section: Numeric scale",
          "text": "A separate fictional display test uses an explicitly defined score from 0 to 10 points, with anchors at 0, 5 and 10 and a sample marker at 7. Units are points. This is an invented UI test scale, not a learner score, actual measurement or empirical distribution. A proportional numeric axis can encode these provided test values; it must not add unprovided uncertainty intervals, thresholds or observations."
        },
        {
          "id": "ref-12",
          "location": "Section: Image answer authoring guidance",
          "text": "The displayed shape is a triangle, and the supplied written rule maps a triangle to LEFT. The correct response is A. Left. This paragraph is answer-bearing authoring guidance. It must never be cited as initial question or neutral stimulus evidence. The unchanged image supplies the actual task rule and unmarked options; a label disclosing the chosen response belongs only after reveal."
        }
      ]
    },
